<% content_for :title, "How to play" %>
<article class="how-to-play prose">
  <h1>How to play</h1>
  <p>
    Polychrome is a tabletop game in the style of the old console RPGs: a GM runs the story, you play one character,
    and fights are menus, like Final Fantasy. If you know D&amp;D, most of it will feel familiar. The words are a bit
    different, and they're all explained at the bottom (and wherever they're dotted like <%= gloss(:abp) %>).
  </p>

  <nav class="sheet-nav" aria-label="Contents">
    <a href="#the-table">The table</a>
    <a href="#fights">Fights</a>
    <a href="#your-character">Your character</a>
    <a href="#towns-and-the-road">Towns and the road</a>
    <a href="#the-words">The words</a>
  </nav>

  <h2 id="the-table">The table</h2>
  <p>
    The table is where the story happens. The GM speaks as the narrator or as the people you meet, and their lines
    appear in the dialogue box. Sometimes the GM puts a <strong>choice</strong> to the party: everyone picks, and the
    GM settles it. Sometimes the GM calls for a <%= gloss(:check) %>: your stat against a difficulty. At Normal,
    someone ordinary for their level has an even chance, and nothing is ever certain.
  </p>
  <p>
    Playing around one TV, or on a call with a shared screen? That's <strong>local co-op</strong>: the GM opens the
    shared screen, you scan its QR code with your phone and pick your character. Your phone becomes your controller.
  </p>

  <h2 id="fights">Fights</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Everyone picks a command, then the round plays out</strong>, fastest first (<%= stat_term("agi") %>).
      There's no waiting for your turn: you choose while everyone else does.</li>
    <li><strong>Attack</strong> hits with your weapon. <strong>Abilities</strong> are your spells and skills and may cost
      <%= gloss(:max_mp) %>. <strong>Item</strong> uses something from the party's bag. <strong>Defend</strong> halves the
      damage you take this round. <strong>Flee</strong> tries to get the whole party away.</li>
    <li>There's usually a timer. If it runs out, you repeat your last command. Want to chat instead?
      Go on <%= gloss(:auto) %>.</li>
    <li>Every move and monster may have a <%= gloss(:type) %>. Fire burns grass, water puts out fire, ghosts ignore
      normal moves. The <%= link_to "type chart", "/types" %> has the lot. Your basic Attack has no type, so it always lands.</li>
    <li>At the end of your rope you might find a <%= gloss(:desperation) %>.</li>
    <li>Confirm a move and a <strong>timing meter</strong> sweeps: stop it on the mark for a Perfect, a quarter harder.
      Missing costs nothing, and you can turn it off.</li>
    <li>Not on the menu? <strong>Try something</strong>: say what you do, the GM rules on it, and you roll for it.
      The dice that decide things show on the board.</li>
    <li>At 0 HP you're KO'd, not dead. A Phoenix Down, a Raise or a temple brings you back.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="your-character">Your character</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Winning fights gives <%= gloss(:exp) %> (your level) and <%= gloss(:abp) %> (your archetype). Levels come often, and
      every stat grows on its own, so there's no paperwork.</li>
    <li>Your <%= gloss(:job) %> is your class, and you can change it between fights. Each archetype keeps its own
      <%= gloss(:job_level) %>, from 1 to 100. Its abilities come at set archetype levels.</li>
    <li>Abilities get stronger the longer you stay in the archetype: that's <%= gloss(:mastery) %>. An ability is at its best
      in the archetype that teaches it, but a mastered one stays strong wherever you take it. A master White Mage who turns
      Fighter still heals better than a brand-new White Mage.</li>
    <li>Every archetype has a <%= gloss(:type) %>: a Black Mage is fire, so water hurts, and a Monk's fists are fighting, so
      they break Skeletons. It also has a <%= gloss(:signature) %> that's always on its menu (a Knight's Cover, a
      Dragoon's Jump) and usually a <%= gloss(:passive) %>. Reach archetype level 100 and the archetype is
      <%= gloss(:mastered) %>: you keep its passive in every archetype.</li>
    <li>Every archetype has a <%= gloss(:field_ability) %> for outside battle: a Thief's Pick Lock, a Dragoon's Scout from Above.
      Ask for it from the table; the GM says yes or no, then you roll. Once per rest.</li>
    <li>Take abilities you learned in one archetype into another with <%= gloss(:ability_slots) %>. A Knight who once studied
      as a White Mage can still cast Cure.</li>
    <li>Gear comes out of the party's bag and goes back into it. The party shares one purse of <%= gloss(:gil) %>.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="towns-and-the-road">Towns and the road</h2>
  <p>
    The GM moves the party on the map, and anyone at the table can say where they'd go under "Where next?": it goes to a
    vote, and the GM settling it takes the party there. The same works for the ways on inside a dungeon. In a town, each building's services open under it: an inn to rest, a temple
    to raise the fallen, a shop, a guild for rumours. They're paid from the party's purse.
  </p>

  <h2 id="the-words">The words</h2>
  <dl class="glossary">
    <% GlossaryHelper::GLOSSARY.each do |key, (label, definition)| %>
      <dt id="term-<%= key %>"><%= label %></dt><dd><%= definition %></dd>
    <% end %>
  </dl>
</article>
